      "latency_ms": 26461.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1205,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Its Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more electronegative (it pulls electrons more strongly) than hydrogen, the electrons in the bond spend more time near the oxygen atom. This gives the oxygen end of the molecule a slight negative charge and the hydrogen ends a slight positive charge. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion).\n\n### 2. Unique Physical Properties\nWater behaves very differently from other common liquids, largely because of its molecular structure:\n*   **Liquid at Room Temperature:** Most compounds made of hydrogen (like hydrogen sulfide) are gases at room temperature. Water is a liquid because of the strong hydrogen bonds between its molecules.\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Unlike most substances, water is most dense as a liquid than as a solid. When water freezes, its molecules form a crystalline structure that takes up more space, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats on lakes, insulating the water below and allowing life to survive in winter.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water has a strong \"skin\" on its surface due to hydrogen bonding, which allows small insects to walk on it.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n\n### 3. The Universal Solvent\nWater is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is due to its polarity; the positive and negative ends of the water molecule attract and pull apart the molecules of other substances. This property is essential for dissolving nutrients in the soil and transporting them into plant roots, as well as for digestion in animals.\n\n### 4. Essential for Life\nWater is the foundation of life on Earth. It is required for:\n*   **Transport:** Moving nutrients and waste through cells and organisms.\n*   **Temperature Regulation:** Sweating and panting rely on water evaporation to cool the body.\n*   **Chemical Reactions:** Many biological reactions (like photosynthesis and cellular respiration) cannot happen without water as a medium.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple molecule ($H_2O$) that, due to its unique structure, creates a liquid environment capable of sustaining complex life on Earth."
